  "order": 14,
  "title": "while loops",
  "summary": "Repeat a block of code as long as a test stays True.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "loop",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "A **loop** repeats code. `while` repeats its indented block **as long as** its test is True:\n\n```\nn = 1\nwhile n <= 3:\n    print(n)\n    n += 1\n```\n\nEach time round, Python checks the test first. True: run the block, then go back up and check again. False: skip the block and carry on below the loop.\n\nSomething in the block must change so the test eventually becomes False. Here `n += 1` does that. If nothing changes, the loop runs forever: an **infinite loop**. These lessons stop such code after a moment; on your own computer you press Ctrl-C.\n\nThe **loop** panel counts the rounds and shows the test's answer each time.",
    "analogy": "Stirring soup while it is still lumpy. Before each stir you look: still lumpy? Stir again. Smooth? Stop and serve. If you never look, you stir forever."
  },
